            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Reconciliation Checklist: Overseas Ad Recharge, Spend &amp; Invoice Alignment</h2>
<h3>Before You Start: Confirm Data Sources</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] <strong>Inventory all accounts</strong>: List all ad platform account IDs and their corresponding business entities (Google Ads, Meta, TikTok, Pinterest, etc.)</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Pull recharge records</strong>: Export top-up transactions from ad platform dashboards, noting date, amount, currency, and payment method</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Export spend reports</strong>: Align reporting periods to calendar weeks or months; watch for timezone and billing cycle differences</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Collect all invoices</strong>: Separate platform-issued invoices from agency-issued invoices and file them separately</li>
</ul>
<h3>Core Reconciliation Steps</h3>
<h4>Step 1: Recharge Amount vs. Account Receipt</h4>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Check</strong>: Did the full recharge amount arrive? Any fees or FX losses?</p>
</blockquote>
<ol>
<li>Compare recharge records against account balance changes in the platform dashboard</li>
<li>For agency-funded recharges, cross-check the agency's payment ledger with platform receipt logs</li>
<li>Flag discrepancies: FX spread, processing fees, failed transactions</li>
</ol>
<h4>Step 2: Spend Data vs. Platform Reports</h4>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Check</strong>: Does reported spend match actual consumption?</p>
</blockquote>
<ol>
<li>Use the platform's settlement report as the source of truth and compare with the ad dashboard spend figures</li>
<li>Adjust for timezone offsets — some platforms settle in UTC; convert to your local time for reporting</li>
<li>Deduct any refunds or balance returns from the total spend figure</li>
</ol>
<h4>Step 3: Invoice Amount vs. Confirmed Spend</h4>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Check</strong>: Do invoice header, amount, and tax rate match the actual spend?</p>
</blockquote>
<ol>
<li>Distinguish between VAT invoices and Proforma Invoices</li>
<li>Verify that the account ID on the invoice matches the recharged account</li>
<li>Confirm whether the invoice amount is inclusive or exclusive of tax for accurate tax filing</li>
</ol>
<h3>Common Discrepancies &amp; Fixes</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Issue</th>
<th>Likely Cause</th>
<th>Recommended Action</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Recharge not received</td>
<td>Payment gateway delay or failure</td>
<td>Contact agency or platform support; keep a written record</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Lower spend reported</td>
<td>Report data still processing</td>
<td>Wait 24-48 hours and re-export</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Invoice amount mismatch</td>
<td>FX fluctuation or promotional credits applied</td>
<td>Use platform settlement statement as reference; request a corrected invoice</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Multi-account summary variance</td>
<td>Different currencies requiring conversion</td>
<td>Standardize to USD or CNY baseline; maintain a FX ledger</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Post-Reconciliation Actions</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Prepare a <strong>Monthly Ad Spend Summary</strong> covering four pillars: recharge, spend, balance, and invoices</li>
<li>[ ] Submit discrepancies for approval with explanations and supporting screenshots or chat records</li>
<li>[ ] Share reconciliation results with the performance marketing team to prevent campaigns from pausing due to insufficient balance</li>
<li>[ ] Conduct quarterly audits of agency recharge channels to verify fund safety</li>
</ul>
<h3>Tools &amp; Efficiency Tips</h3>
<ol>
<li>Use Google Sheets or Excel templates with embedded formulas to reduce manual calculation errors</li>
<li>Teams with resources can connect BI tools via API to auto-generate reconciliation reports</li>
<li>Negotiate a reconciliation schedule with your agency, specifying invoice issuance timelines (typically 7-15 business days after spend confirmation)</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<p><strong>Quick 3-Point Verification</strong>:<br />
1. Did the recharge arrive?<br />
2. Does spend match platform reports?<br />
3. Does the invoice amount reconcile?</p>
<p>All three confirmed — reconciliation is closed.</p>
